{
  "id": "25-region-falls",
  "chapter": "Edge",
  "title": "The Region falls",
  "brief": "A whole Region, a data centre with all its power and network, goes down 20 seconds into the wave. Everything you built in Region 1 fails at once. You need a standby Region: a second load balancer, app servers and a Read Replica in Region 2, always on and paid for. Put a DNS in front of both Regions. It sends the users to Region 1, and when Region 1 fails it switches them to Region 2, but only after its TTL has passed. Until then every request fails, and your reputation falls fast. A shorter TTL switches sooner and costs more. When the DNS switches, the Read Replica in Region 2 becomes the primary database (failover). Use the Build in switch to build in Region 2, and connect the DNS to both Regions.",
  "requirements": {
    "p95Ms": 300,
    "maxErrorRate": 0.01
  },
  "allowedComponents": [
    "dns",
    "loadBalancer",
    "appServer",
    "database",
    "readReplica"
  ],
  "componentOptions": {
    "appServer": {
      "sizes": [
        "medium"
      ],
      "maxInstances": 2
    },
    "database": {
      "sizes": [
        "small"
      ]
    },
    "readReplica": {
      "sizes": [
        "small"
      ],
      "maxInstances": 1
    }
  },
  "guidedHint": "When a whole Region fails, requests keep going to it until the DNS switches the users, and that takes the TTL. Your reputation falls about 6% each second until then, so you cannot wait 30 seconds. Select the DNS and choose a shorter TTL: 10 seconds is enough, 5 costs more. The Read Replica in Region 2 becomes the primary database when the DNS switches.",
